   "date": "2014-05-13T06:22:27Z",
   "subject": "Re: [r-inla] Using Inla in the prescence of disconnected components",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "On Sun, 2014-05-11 at 04:35 -0700, Mahbubeh Parsaeian wrote:\r<br>&gt; Hi INLA team \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; I want to run a Besag model at country level in the Word shape file. I\r<br>&gt; have 101 islands in my data set and when I run the model the warning\r<br>&gt; about” 106 connected components” appears. I have searched about this\r<br>&gt; problem and understood that the graph consists of 106 disconnected sub\r<br>&gt; graphs and I should change the adjust.for.con.comp = FALSE where TRUE\r<br>&gt; is default. My problem is that when I change this argument, the\r<br>&gt; predicted values are approximately equal to the observed data, I mean\r<br>&gt; the correlation between the predicted values and observed data are\r<br>&gt; about 1. I run a simple regression model to see how the results are\r<br>&gt; different from simple regression. The result is as follows. \r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "Hi,\r<br>\r<br>there is an implicite assumption in the besag-model that the graph is\r<br>connected and the question is what to do when its not. the default\r<br>behaviour is to use a hierarchical model with one besag-model for each\r<br>connected component but with a common precision parameter. if you set\r<br>adjust.for.con.comp=FALSE, then no corrections is made (except the rank\r<br>deficiancy), but this might lead to an improper posterior.\r<br>\r<br>if you want, I can have a look at the results if you send me the graph\r<br>and data and how to run it, so I can provide some more insight. \r<br>\r<br>Best\r<br>H\r<br>\r<br>-- \r<br>Håvard Rue\r<br><a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">he...@r-inla.org</a>\r<br>\r<br>"
    ]
   ]
  }
 ]
}
